AU  - Lammey, C.
AU  - Frangieh, J.
AU  - Humphrey, K.G.
AU  - Lehmann, J.
AU  - Thompson, B.
AU  - Hughes, V.
T2  - Nurse Education Today
AB  - Background: Meaningful, formative feedback from faculty to students in the online academic setting promotes the faculty-student relationship and encourages learning. Aim: The aim of this review is to determine the best practices for providing student feedback from faculty in an online setting. Design: Integrative review of the literature encompassing both quantitative and qualitative studies. Data sources: Comprehensive search of four databases: PubMed, Embase, Cumulative Index to Nursing and Allied Health Literature (CINAHL), and Education Resource Information Center (ERIC), limited to articles published between 2014 and 2024. Review methods: Articles were included that reported on faculty-student feedback practices in the undergraduate level or above. Results: Of the 362 articles screened, thirteen were deemed appropriate for this review. Themes explored from the results were multimodal feedback approaches, faculty proficiency in technology, timely feedback practices, content of feedback, and student engagement in feedback. Conclusions: Nurse faculty members who are well-educated on best practices of providing formative feedback to students in an online setting are best equipped to mentor and teach students in modern academic settings. Audiovisual feedback is well received by students and promotes connectedness between students and faculty. Technological tools in the learning management system, such as screen capture recordings and tracking feedback clicks, may be useful in providing feedback in the online setting. Student engagement with feedback and the level of student feedback literacy were found to be important aspects of the feedback process. Future research should focus on student feedback literacy and the use of artificial intelligence in faculty-to-student feedback. © 2024 Elsevier Ltd
