TI  - Meet Zara: An AI-generated teaching assistant for enhancing formative assessment and self-regulated learning
AU  - Eager, B.
AB  - This case study illustrates how novel artificial intelligence (AI) text-to-video capabilities were leveraged to enhance formative assessment and self-regulated learning for students enrolled in a third-year undergraduate module of a bachelor of business program at an Australian university. The module was designed to guide students in planning future careers, emphasising entrepreneurial pathways. The initiative featured an AI-generated teaching assistant named Zara who provided instructional content and insights into the practicalities of entrepreneurial careers. Zara was crafted to be a relatable character who not only taught but also applied the module’s learning materials, thus serving as a reflective tool for students to evaluate their own entrepreneurial aspirations and strategies – an approach that capitalises on the influential role of relatable role models in fostering entrepreneurial intentions. The aim was to enrich students’ comprehension of the module content and to stimulate active engagement, thereby enhancing their ability to apply academic concepts to real-world contexts and complete associated assessments. The case study demonstrates that AI-generated video content can offer a dynamic and interactive formative assessment experience, suggesting that AI text-to-video technology offers promise for enhancing student learning outcomes and embodies a modern pedagogical approach that resonates with contemporary digital learning environments. © 2025 selection and editorial matter, Sam Elkington and Alastair Irons.
