TI  - Was it Active Learning all Along?: Investigating the Effectiveness of the Mode of Exposure to Bloom's Taxonomy-Based Assignments in an Undergraduate Fluid Mechanics Course
AU  - Charoenphol, P.
AU  - Kim, H.
AU  - Bandyopadhyay, A.
T2  - Proceedings - Frontiers in Education Conference, FIE
AB  - This work-in-progress paper assesses the effectiveness of the mode of delivery of minimally-modified assignments with Bloom's Taxonomy-based questions on students' performance in formative and summative assessments and the overall student learning experience. We compare four student groups from three consecutive semesters in a large undergraduate fluid mechanics course: two groups (TRAD and TRAD+BT), who were taught using traditional lecture/instructor-centered mode, and two other groups (AL and AL+BT), who were taught in an active learning classroom with various in-class activities (student-centered), including anonymous online polling and discussion-based assignments. Additionally, TRAD+BT students were exposed to Bloom's taxonomy questions incorporated as part of the lecture slides while AL+BT students worked on in-class activities covering questions at five Bloom's taxonomy cognitive levels: remember, understand, apply, analyze, and evaluate. Preliminary results suggest that students in the active learning environments performed better than the students in the instructor-centered classrooms in the formative assessments. However, the exposure to Bloom's Taxonomy questions in either setting did not significantly enhance student grades. Similarly, the active learners performed markedly better than the traditional classroom students in the summative assessment (midterm exam). In this case, the exposure to Bloom's Taxonomy questions further enhanced the performance of the active learning students from AL+BT. Overall, our present findings suggest that an active learning environment could be integral to ensuring that Bloom's Taxonomy-based assignments are able to promote student learning. An end-of-semester survey was conducted using an online learning management system to evaluate student learning experiences and metacognition as a result of this teaching innovation. © 2023 IEEE.
