AB  - The proceedings contain 1770 papers. The topics discussed include: a framework for developing a deeper understanding of the factors that influence success and failure in undergraduate engineering capstone design experiences; a real-time attendance system using deep learning face recognition; an investigation of when and where ethics appears in undergraduate engineering curricula; bioreactor design, automation and optimization - a multidisciplinary approach; challenges of developing a new engineering drawings course for civil engineers; enhancing middle/high school female students self-confidence and motivation in pursuing STEM careers through increasing diversity in engineering and labor-force (IDEAL) outreach summer program; evaluation of evidence-based teaching techniques in a graduate fluid dynamics course; integration of service learning to teaching thermodynamics; measuring students’ engagement in learning volumes of revolution when using advanced visualization media in an active learning environment; participatory action research (PAR) as formative assessment of a stem summer bridge program; and the broader impacts of an additive manufacturing course at three large universities.
