TI  - Combining Pre-class Preparation with Collaborative In-Class Activities to Improve Student Engagement and Success in General Chemistry
AU  - Blaser, M.
T2  - ACS Symposium Series
AB  - A large and growing body of evidence indicates that active learning pedagogy (ALP) methods are, broadly speaking, more effective than traditional instruction (Freeman, S. Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. 2014, 111, 8410-8415. PNAS 2014, 111, 8410-8415). However, ALP is still not widely used in college chemistry instruction (Stains, M. Science 2018, 359, 1468-1470). In addition, ALP methods are quite heterogeneous, and (much) less is known about the effects of particular ALP approaches. During the past five years, a flipped classroom approach incorporating multiple active learning methods has been successfully used to increase student engagement and improve student learning outcomes in General Chemistry courses at Shasta College. This chapter will focus on the two central aspects of the course transformation: (i) promoting targeted student preparation before class; and (ii) facilitating meaningful follow-up collaborative learning activities during class. In this context pre-class preparation consists primarily of viewing instructor-created screencasts on foundational concepts and material and completing pre-class quizzes that pertain to the topics in the screencasts. In-class class time is then structured around having students work both individually and collaboratively on central concepts and (more) challenging problem-solving, using a modified form of peer instruction, with a bring-your-own-device student response system to track student progress and as formative assessment. Although both interventions appear to be beneficial to student engagement and learning, there is reason to believe the combination of these two methods may have a larger positive effect (Rau, M. A. J. Chem. Educ. 2017, 94, 1404-1414). © 2019 American Chemical Society. All rights reserved.
