TY  - JOUR
TI  - Active Learning in the Large Lecture Hall Format
AU  - Labrake, C.
T2  - ACS Symposium Series
AB  - In 2011, The University of Texas at Austin administrators challenged departments offering large enrollment, lower division service courses to transform their courses to modern student-centered active learning environments. The goals of this course transformation program were to increase learning outcomes and student throughput. Moving from the lecture format teaching 350 to 500 students per section to using active methods seemed, to many, an impossible task. On the contrary, creating an engaging, inviting, safe space to actively learn general chemistry in a large lecture format is possible. We found success via collaboration which included careful planning, an iterative development process resulting in well-developed materials, shared resources and institutional support. Here the steps the development team took to move from teaching mostly using didactic lecture format to an active, student-centered learning environment will be outlined. These are: (I) Developing in-class active learning activities with suggested polling questions, and the creative use of a classroom response system; (II) Developing the Learning Assistant program, complete with a new training course which includes modules on leadership and ethics and a vertically aligned train the trainer model; and (III) Development of a robust out-of-class rich e-book which houses videos, text and images and is chunked and metered out to students via Canvas learning modules with embedded formative assessment questions, and is shared by all faculty teaching the course. Upon observation of one of our active learning courses, one will generally see the students engaged in informal small group work supported by Learning Assistants. © 2019 American Chemical Society. All rights reserved.
