TI  - Online Writing Feedback at University: Benefits and Impact on Undergraduate Students’ Academic Writing Literacy
AU  - Chepinchikj, N.
AU  - Wu, J.
AU  - Gardiner, G.
T2  - Scopus Preprints
AB  - This study examines the pedagogical value and limitations of an external online assessment feedback service, within a first-year undergraduate social sciences course at an Australian university. Using data from 2020 to 2022, we conducted a qualitative analysis of tutor feedback, supplemented by student surveys and grade comparisons. Our findings show that online feedback offers detailed, constructive guidance, combining technical and substantive commentary with a supportive tone. Students benefit from the ability to request specific feedback areas, while tutors often prioritise more pressing issues, enhancing relevance and impact. The feedback encourages active student engagement and supports academic writing development. However, inconsistencies in feedback categorisation and time constraints on tutors limit the quality and depth. Nonetheless, these services are particularly valuable in contexts where academic staff face time pressures and limited consultation opportunities. This paper contributes to the literature on feedback literacy and digital pedagogy by demonstrating how online feedback can supplement institutional practices and promote formative learning. In a rapidly evolving AI landscape where AI-generated feedback is passively consumed, we advocate for a more integrated and reflective approach to assessment feedback that aligns with student-centred learning, intellectual curiosity and critical thinking.
DA  - 2026///
PY  - 2026
