AB  - Manual grading of short answer questions in higher education is time consuming and often relies on simplified methods that do not fully capture the conceptual understanding of students. These limitations can reduce the effectiveness of formative assessment and lower the pedagogical value of open ended tasks, particularly in courses that emphasize reasoning and explanation. Scalable alternatives are required to maintain evaluation depth without increasing instructor workload. In this paper, we introduce EvaluAI, an automatic grading tool powered by Large Language Models (GPT-4o mini), enhanced through advanced prompting strategies including Zero-shot, Few-shot, Chain of Thought (CoT), and Knowledge Integration. Using a modular prompt design and a differential evolution algorithm, EvaluAI maps GPT-generated scores (0–10) to a standardized 0–1 scale via piecewise linear functions, minimizing mean squared error (MSE) and aligning with expert evaluations. EvaluAI was tested on short-answer constructed-response questions in a second-year Data Structures course with over 130 students, achieving a coefficient of determination (R2 = 0.60) and error levels comparable to those of human experts. It also demonstrated adaptability by accurately evaluating responses in an Optimization course, highlighting its potential as a scalable and pedagogically informed assessment tool applicable across a wide range of subjects in higher education. The code and experiments are available at: \url{http://github.com/OscarRojasG/Experimentos-EvaluAI}
