TI  - Emerging Technologies in Higher Education Assessment and Feedback Practices: A Systematic Literature Review
AU  - Sembey, R.
AU  - Hoda, R.
AU  - Grundy, J.
T2  - Scopus Preprints
AB  - The use of Emerging Technologies (ETs), such as Artificial Intelligence, Learning Analytics (LA) and Extended Reality (XR), in higher education (HE) has proliferated in recent times, as these technologies are considered to have significant impact on the future of postsecondary teaching and learning. We wanted to find out ETs used in computing education, effectiveness of use, and gaps for future research. We carried out Systematic Literature Review on ETs to identify the state of the art in the use of these technologies for HE computing education assessment and feedback practices. We selected 38 articles for detailed meta-analysis through systematic search and filtering of 3038 studies published between 2016 and 2021. Findings reveal that 71% of the reviewed studies are journal articles, 50% studies focus on LA, and majority employ quantitative approaches. The results suggest that XR technologies have received least attention and there is a lack of frameworks for design, use and evaluation of ETs in HE. We also present a brief research agenda, drawing attention to the need to trial more XR, focus on formative assessment and feedback practices, better understand impact of human-centric issues and more thoughtful consideration of ethics in the use of ETs in computing education.
