TI  - Exploring the impact of ChatGPT on module-level assessments: a case study in Hong Kong
AU  - Chen, Linsey
AU  - Cheng, Andy
T2  - LAW TEACHER
AB  - The advent of ChatGPT brought significant changes to teaching and learning experiences in AI-driven environments. However, what can be done at the module level to respond to the opportunities and challenges associated with ChatGPT has rarely been addressed. This study provides a nuanced picture of the impact of ChatGPT on module-level assessments. This paper examines changes implemented in assessment design; carries out empirical experiments to evaluate the performance of ChatGPT-generated assignments and conducts a survey to assess students' perceptions of ChatGPT's impact on assessments. Moreover, focused issues are explored within a broader policy context. It is advocated that formative assessment is a desirable assessment type in the post-ChatGPT era and its proportion should be increased at module level due to its benefits in promoting learning effectiveness, addressing concerns related to academic integrity, and bridging the gap between workplaces and universities. This case study puts the theory into practice; and the findings from this case study indicate that students respond positively to such change at module level. Our research innovation is also rooted in combining survey, empirical experiments, and presenting through thematic maps to show an overall picture of the potential impact of employing ChatGPT on module-level assessment.
