AU  - Zhao, Ruoyi
AU  - Li, Dongli
T2  - TEACHING AND LEARNING IN NURSING
AB  - Background: In the era of evolving nursing discipline and the "Double Million" plan, nursing undergraduate education faces new challenges. Current teaching quality assessment standards need a more refined training index system. The OBE concept and hybrid education model present innovative ways to enhance nursing education quality. Aim: This research endeavors to develop a nursing course formative evaluation system grounded in the OBE concept through scientific research methods, to offer a foundation for evaluating the quality of undergraduate nursing talent cultivation. Methods: Literature was analyzed by scouring multiple databases. Semi-structured interviews with nursing experts were conducted, followed by Delphi expert consultations. Statistical tools like SPSS 26.0 and Yaahp 10.3 software were utilized for data analysis. Results: Twelve experts participated in 2 rounds of letter consultations, with response rates of 92.3% and 100%, and authority coefficients of 0.862 and 0.887. After index screening, the finalized system consisted of 4 first-level, 17 second-level, and 73 third-level indicators. Conclusions: The established formative evaluation index is valid, providing a basis for nursing curriculum formative evaluation. (c) 2025 Organization for Associate Degree Nursing. Published by Elsevier Inc. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
