TI  - Classification of Kinematics Problems Using Artificial Intelligence for Design Tests in Physics Education in Engineering
AU  - J. Catana-Castellanos
AU  - E. Cervantes-Juárez
AU  - D. Sanchez-Guzman
AU  - G. Gallegos
T2  - 2025 IEEE Frontiers in Education Conference (FIE)
AB  - This research WIP paper describes the development of a digital tool aimed at enhancing the teaching and assessment of kinematics in engineering education. The system is based on a database of problems extracted from widely used physics textbooks and classified through Artificial Intelligence (AI) techniques. Each problem, formatted as natural-language text, is processed by the classifier to assign a difficulty level based on four main dimensions: mathematical complexity, number of solution steps, interpretative difficulty, and level of analytical reasoning. These dimensions are derived from a custom rubric created in collaboration with physics educators, including the notion level of abstraction, defined as the degree to which a problem requires conceptual versus procedural understanding. The classifier generates labeled outputs that categorize the problems according to difficulty. An adaptive system is being implemented to adjust difficulty levels based on student performance, creating a feedback loop for continuous improvement. A web platform is also under development to support automatic problem selection for exams or practice and to allow instructors to upload and categorize new problems. Future stages will involve the deployment of pre- and post-tests aligned with the LSEESC didactic model. These tests will be validated through statistical metrics such as Concentration Factor, Normalized Conceptual Gain, and the Rasch Index. The goal of this initiative is to provide a validated framework for problem classification that supports equitable, scalable, and personalized learning trajectories-paving the way for AI-integrated instruction in physics education.
