TI  - Longitudinal Evaluation of Student Writing Improvement Using Human-in-the-Loop Open-SLLMs for Automated Scoring and Revision
AU  - G. Sunitha
AU  - M. S. Vaishnavi
AU  - V. Joshitha
AU  - M. Rajesh
AU  - V. A. Phani
AU  - K. Amala
T2  - 2025 4th International Conference on Applied Artificial Intelligence and Computing (ICAAIC)
AB  - Expanding on the previous findings indicating that open-source large language models (LLMs) have moderate human-machine agreement in automated essay scoring (AES) and good performance in automated essay revising (AER), the present study goes further to estimate the pedagogical effect of the LLM-assisted writing feedback. In a longitudinal study of 1, 200 students' essays, our response system embedded ChatGLM2-6B in a few-shot and p-tuning setup into a human-in-the-loop feedback system. Quantitative analysis demonstrated that the group with the help of the LLM improved their average score in AES by 24.8 (p < 0.01) and demonstrated the average similarity of the text in the texts with 0.84, which signifies stylistic retention. The frequency of student self-revision increased (41 percent), and the time of grading was also decreased (63 percent) compared to the control group of traditional teacher feedback-assisted revisions. The results indicate that open-source LLM can be used as scalable, secure, and pedagogic tools of continuous writing assessment. The next step in the future will be to investigate adaptive prompt optimization and personalized feedback generation in various genres and languages.
