AU  - J. Chen
AU  - C. Chan
T2  - 2025 IEEE International Conference on Teaching, Assessment, and Learning for Engineering (TALE)
AB  - Oral presentations in English are of significant importance for students in higher education; however, obtaining timely and effective feedback during practice remains a challenge. At the same time, educators face increasing workloads, making it difficult to provide students with prompt, personalized feedback on their presentation skills. The advent of Artificial Intelligence (AI) offers promising solutions for both students and teachers by enabling the delivery of rapid and individualized feedback. Nonetheless, while AI is capable of addressing many aspects of feedback, it often lacks the ability to interpret nuanced elements of human communication, such as tone, body language, and cultural context. To overcome this limitation, a hybrid approach that integrates AI with human expertise has been adopted. In this project, a rubric-based platform called NinjOrAItor has been developed to implement this approach, aiming to deliver timely, tailored feedback to students while alleviating the feedback burden on instructors. Preliminary evaluations indicate that NinjOrAItor not only enhances the quality and timeliness of feedback but also supports student confidence and skill development in oral English presentations. In a database subject with 171 students (predominantly English as a Foreign Language (EFL) learners), participants achieved a 4.80% improvement in presentation scores.
