TI  - Exploring student acceptance of a new tool for AI-generated formative feedback on report writing
AU  - J. H. Ling Wong
AU  - C. Ching Lee
AU  - D. Y. Chua
T2  - 2025 IEEE International Conference on Teaching, Assessment, and Learning for Engineering (TALE)
AB  - As generative AI tools become increasingly prevalent in higher education, this paper explores the use of an LLM-based workflow system developed in-house to provide AI-generated feedback on technical report writing. FeedbackAId aims to supplement peer and instructor reviews by giving effective formative feedback to students. A mixed-methods approach was employed across two studies to evaluate the perceptions of FeedbackAId among engineering students who were using it for the first time. The system was built using readily available software such as Microsoft Excel, Power Automate, and OpenAI’s GPT-4o mini to offer scalable feedback with minimal cost. Students found the tool useful and the feedback to be constructive. About 90% expressed willingness to use FeedbackAId again. Technology Acceptance Model (TAM) metrics – including Perceived Ease of Use (PEOU), Perceived Usefulness (PU), Perceived Intelligence (PI), and Perceived Trustworthiness (PT) – all scored 80% and above. Students requested improvements such as more detailed feedback and the ability to ask follow-up questions. Prompt refinement based on student input improved tool performance, underscoring the role of prompt engineering by the instructor in enhancing outcomes. Overall, both studies indicate student acceptance of AI-generated feedback, suggesting that FeedbackAId can be effectively integrated into writing pedagogy to complement instructor input.
