TI  - Adaptive Learning Models for Personalized Literary Education: A Hybrid AI Approach
AU  - S. I. L
AU  - M. M P
AU  - S. S
AU  - R. TS
AU  - R. Manimuthukumar
AU  - S. R
T2  - 2025 IEEE 5th International Conference on ICT in Business Industry & Government (ICTBIG)
AB  - In recent years, there has been a growing interest in individualized pedagogies in literary study as adaptive learning environments facilitated by artificial intelligence (AI) have gained traction. Traditional instructional pedagogies and approaches are adept to bypass noticing learners' differences in researching knowledge comprehension, pacing, and motivational engagement levels. In this project we present an integrated, AI-enabled adaptive learning framework comparing predictive analytics with established cognitive theories for learning and engagement. The framework utilizes an ongoing, multimodal assessment of comprehension proxies, interactional reading traces, and prior knowledge schemata to continuously curate literary texts, Socratic questions or prompts, and structured scaffolding for individuals based on their evolving learner profiles. Based on a systematic literature review, a number of clear gaps persist in the field - name) under-contextualized pathways, rigid feedback loops, and disconnects from cognitive instructional principles. For the prototype, a concurrent mixed-methods design triangulated the statistical distillation outcomes to qualitative interview and annotation data acquired from a sample of secondary students. Compared to the controls we observed statistically significant levels of improvement in all areas investigated including comprehension test scores, selfdirected reading behaviours, and participatory equity. In tandem, teacher respondents noted a perceived decrease in instructional burden with enhanced assessment for learning, and again in equal measure a perception of greater curriculum entitlement. The results evidenced here spotlight an emergent capability for a hybrid AI framework to develop individualized, meaningful instructional conversations in literary studies, and we advocate for their systematic engagement into future curricular planning and pedagogical regulation.
