TI  - Modeling Student Improvement in Tests with Multiple Attempts
AU  - A. Boicu
T2  - 2023 International Conference on Machine Learning and Applications (ICMLA)
AB  - Formative assessments are crucial elements of the educational process. The number of attempts offered on academic assessments varies greatly and can significantly impact student learning. This study explores the association between overall student learning outcomes and the number of attempts allowed on formative tests. Identifying the ideal number of attempts will minimize wasted time while encouraging student learning. Moreover, determining an individual and student-specific ideal number of attempts may be more beneficial than calculating a course-wide number. Prior research on the effect of multiple attempts has yielded results varying from a notable to a negligible difference in scores between attempts. This study examined data from an introductory computer science course and developed a variety machine learning models to predict whether students would significantly improve their scores if they took another attempt on an assessment. Similarly, to other machine learning models used to model student performance, the models achieved accuracy scores between 0.7 and 0.8. However, these relatively low average accuracies emphasize the need for a personalized approach to attempt optimization.
