AB  - Innovative approaches and new technological practices in education have changed the way of teaching and learning, and are now becoming more and more important, especially with growing numbers of students in universities and institutions. The development of an automated system capable of assessing and grading students’ UML class diagrams could be very beneficial for any tutor to assess a larger number of students in an efficient way and at a lower cost. This paper presents a new approach for assessing and grading students’ UML diagrams. The method consists of finding similarities between the student’s diagram and the tutor’s diagram as a solution, then providing the student’s score and detailed guidance based on the differences found. To attain this objective, we have developed an algorithm that compares two XMI files obtained from the transformation of the UML class diagrams, then provides the student’s score and the differences found between the elements of the two diagrams.
