TI  - Central Dogma of Biology: Inclusion, Educational Game, and AI Technology in the University Classroom
AU  - J. A. Yunes-Rojas
AU  - J. M. Nieto-Jalil
AU  - H. A. Zaldivar
AU  - I. C. Solís
AU  - J. Lozano-Aponte
T2  - 2025 Mexican International Conference on Computer Science (ENC)
AB  - We present a low-cost, UDL-grounded instructional approach to teaching the central dogma of molecular biology in an undergraduate course. The design integrates a kinesthetic MDF jigsaw with PLA 3D-printed molecular models, multimodal accessibility assets (Braille process cards, captioned/narrated micro-lectures, QR-linked resources), and an educational chatbot that delivers formative, rationale-seeking feedback on student-generated case studies. In a pilot cohort ($N=18$; matched $n=10$), learning scores increased from 8.2 (pre) to $9.6 / 10$ (post) on a paired test, $\mathrm{t}(9)=2.91$, two-tailed p $=0.017,95 \% \text{CI}$ for $\Delta \mathrm{M}=1.4:[0.31,2.49]$; variance decreased from 3.07 to 0.27, indicating greater response consistency. Standardized effects were large (paired $\mathbf{d}_{(\mathbf{z})}=0.92$; Hedges' $\mathbf{g} \approx$ 0.98). Perception data showed impact/usefulness $=69.7 / 100$ (collaborative work $=94$) and engagement $=75.5 / 100$ (self-regulation/strategic thinking $=89$). Grounded in Universal Design for Learning and research on serious games and chatbots in education, the approach is scalable and transferable to related life-science topics, providing a transferable framework for inclusive STEM education. We outline threats to validity (sample size, instrumentation) and a replication plan with pre-registered analyses and component ablation to benchmark the relative contribution of the tactile kit, multimodal media, and chatbot.
