TY  - CONF
TI  - Correlating Cognitive Abilities with Programming Proficiency: A Basis for Optimized Sectioning in Educational Settings
AU  - G. P. Brazil
AU  - C. E. Dumdumaya
T2  - 2025 IEEE International Conference on Emerging Trends in Engineering and Computing (ETECOM)
AB  - This study examines the relationship between cognitive abilities, specifically intelligence quotient (IQ), and programming proficiency among first-year college students. Using correlation analysis, clustering, progression tracking, and predictive modeling, it explores whether IQ alone can predict performance and guide instructional sectioning. Results showed weak and insignificant correlations between IQ and most programming components, with only a moderate negative relationship found for midterm grades. K-Means clustering based on IQ, SHS strand, gender, and age revealed distinct performance profiles, while progression analysis indicated no significant link between IQ and improvement from Programming 1 to 2. A Random Forest model excluding IQ achieved an R2 of 0.7871, identifying midterm, lab, and practical scores as the strongest predictors of final grades. Overall, the findings demonstrate that IQ alone is not a reliable determinant of programming success; integrating behavioral, demographic, and continuous assessment data offers a more equitable and data-driven approach to student sectioning in programming education.
