TI  - Adaptive Intelligence: AI-Powered Personalized Learning and Dynamic Assessments
AU  - M. Abrar
AU  - W. Aboraya
AU  - K. P. Subramanian
AU  - R. Abdulghafor
AU  - Y. A. Husaini
AU  - M. A. Husaini
T2  - 2025 International Conference on Electrical, Communication and Computer Engineering (ICECCE)
AB  - In education, artificial intelligence (AI) innovation has also been embraced in several ways, especially in areas of dynamic assessment and personalized learning. Traditional education models can hardly address the differences between students and their abilities; the requirement for AI-based adaptive learning models exists because of it. The research explores how to aid smart learning pathways together with dynamic assessment systems to improve learning efficiency. It examines the potential of AI to enhance the acquisition of knowledge, decrease time for learningrelated tasks, and increase students' interest. The study also seeks to compare the above AI-based approaches with the conventional teaching methods. A quasi-experimental study was carried out for six weeks for both groups with 100 students each, where one group used an AI-based system for learning, whereas the other group used the conventional system. The assessments of knowledge improvement and time optimization were attained using pre and post-tests, as well as constant analyses of the results and engagement data. T-tests were conducted as a part of hypothesis testing to analyze performance and engagement between these groups. The AI-based learning system led experimental subjects to gain 25% better results than the 14% achieved by the control group. Learning tasks of the experimental group required less time to complete by 25%, while their engagement levels rose by 15% above the control group students. The data demonstrates how AI technology improves personalized education through customized learning programs and prompt feedback, thus advancing student achievement. Research on these matters demands larger participant numbers, including advanced education levels, while assessing the consistent impacts of educational AI assistance on the skill retention and knowledge improvement of students.
