AU  - M. -A. -E. Pascu
AU  - I. -L. Cioata
AU  - M. Raschip
T2  - 2025 IEEE 37th International Conference on Tools with Artificial Intelligence (ICTAI)
AB  - Finding minimal-size sorting networks is a challenging optimization problem with practical applications, as it leads to more efficient parallel systems. In this paper, we present an algorithm based on reinforcement learning for constructing minimal-size sorting networks. Several versions of the algorithm, based on the Q-learning method, were proposed. A baseline method, a redundancy-aware variant, a forward check strategy that filters ineffective actions, and a replay buffer approach for improved learning stability are among the versions that have been investigated. The forward check strategy achieves the best results by discovering optimal solutions for up to 8 inputs and producing near-optimal solutions for 9 and 10 inputs. Overall, the results are encouraging and suggest that reinforcement learning could represent a valuable direction for developing efficient techniques in the design of sorting networks.
