TI  - Best practices for providing meaningful faculty-student feedback in an online setting: An integrative review applied to nursing education.
AU  - Lammey, Christina
AU  - Frangieh, Jihane
AU  - Humphrey, Katherine G.
AU  - Lehmann, Julia
AU  - Thompson, Brett
AU  - Hughes, Vickie
T2  - Nurse education today
AB  - BACKGROUND: Meaningful, formative feedback from faculty to students in the online academic setting promotes the faculty-student relationship and encourages  learning. AIM: The aim of this review is to determine the best practices for  providing student feedback from faculty in an online setting. DESIGN: Integrative  review of the literature encompassing both quantitative and qualitative studies.  DATA SOURCES: Comprehensive search of four databases: PubMed, Embase, Cumulative  Index to Nursing and Allied Health Literature (CINAHL), and Education Resource  Information Center (ERIC), limited to articles published between 2014 and 2024.  REVIEW METHODS: Articles were included that reported on faculty-student feedback  practices in the undergraduate level or above. RESULTS: Of the 362 articles  screened, thirteen were deemed appropriate for this review. Themes explored from  the results were multimodal feedback approaches, faculty proficiency in  technology, timely feedback practices, content of feedback, and student  engagement in feedback. CONCLUSIONS: Nurse faculty members who are well-educated  on best practices of providing formative feedback to students in an online  setting are best equipped to mentor and teach students in modern academic  settings. Audiovisual feedback is well received by students and promotes  connectedness between students and faculty. Technological tools in the learning  management system, such as screen capture recordings and tracking feedback  clicks, may be useful in providing feedback in the online setting. Student  engagement with feedback and the level of student feedback literacy were found to  be important aspects of the feedback process. Future research should focus on  student feedback literacy and the use of artificial intelligence in  faculty-to-student feedback.
