AU  - Marras, A.
AU  - Fornara, F.
AU  - Pasqualotto, A.
AB  - The integration of generative artificial intelligence (AI) tools in primary education presents transformative opportunities to foster inclusive learning environments, particularly for students with special educational needs (SENs). By aligning with Universal Design for Learning (UDL) principles, this chapter presents AI as a key driver for personalized learning, meeting diverse student needs, and fostering inclusion. Specific AI tools like large language models (LLMs) and Intelligent Tutoring Systems (ITSs) are highlighted for their role in creating adaptive, individualized learning experiences. The chapter emphasizes the critical need for teacher training in the thoughtful integration of AI and explores teacher training strategies within the Technological Pedagogical Content Knowledge (TPACK) framework, focusing on equipping educators with the necessary technological, pedagogical (TPK), and contextual knowledge (XK) to effectively implement AI-based tools in the classroom. Additionally, challenges such as data privacy, unequal access to technology, and the risk of over-reliance on AI are addressed, noting how these factors could disrupt essential teacher–student and peer interactions. A balanced and responsible approach is advocated, with comprehensive teacher training as the foundation for leveraging AI to support inclusive and holistic student development in primary education. © 2026 John Wiley & Sons, Inc. Published 2026 by John Wiley & Sons, Inc.
