TI  - AI-Augmented Pedagogy: Enhancing Literary Analysis Skills
AU  - Pebbili, R.D.
AU  - Mathews, S.C.S.
T2  - AIP Conference Proceedings
AB  - The present study examines the ability of Artificial Intelligence (AI) to transform undergraduate English literature teaching, with an emphasis on improving literary analysis abilities. Employing a sample of thirty undergraduate students and a quantitative research methodology, the study incorporates virtual writing assistants and AI-powered writing analysis tools into the curriculum. Over the duration of a semester, the objective is to evaluate the effects of these interventions on how students interact with literary texts, writing ability, and literary analysis skills. Students which were exposed to AI treatments showed a noteworthy improvement in their writing competence and literary analysis skills. Virtual writing assistants offer specific assistance in literary analysis, while Automatic Writing Analysis (AWA) methods give students immediate feedback to improve their writing skills. The distinctive significance of this study within the particular context of teaching English literature is highlighted by comparisons with previous studies. Significant practical implications stem from the findings. Pedagogical methods may be enhanced through educational institutions, instructors can use AI tools to develop creative thinking and writing skills, and policymakers can see the advantages of AI-augmented pedagogy in preparing students for the workforce of the future. The study ends by reiterating the benefits of AI-augmented education as a dynamic and successful method of improving literary analysis skills and arguing in favour of integrating these advances in technology into the bigger picture of teaching English literature. © 2025 American Institute of Physics Inc.. All rights reserved.
