TI  - Influence of higher education students’ perceived behaviour on their artificial intelligence acceptance: an empirical investigation using technology acceptance model
AU  - Karan, B.
AU  - Chakma, C.
T2  - Journal of Applied Research in Higher Education
AB  - Purpose: This study aimed to investigate higher education students’ perceived behaviour towards artificial intelligence (AI) acceptance in their learning. Design/methodology/approach: Theory of technology acceptance model (TAM) and its critical constructs were used to develop research model and hypotheses. The research model was validated using structural equation modelling (SEM) based on 405 useable survey responses. Findings: The findings report that the critical constructs of TAM theory are helpful to explain higher education students’ perceived behaviour towards their AI acceptance in learning. The findings also confirm the reliability and strength of TAM theory to assess higher education students’ behavioural intention to accept AI. Practical implications: The findings will help authorities of higher education to understand the students’ behavioural intention to accept AI. The study will assist authorities, policymakers, designers, developers and decision-makers of higher education to facilitate the effective integration of AI in higher education. Originality/value: The study contributes to enhance new knowledge in literature regarding AI acceptance in higher education. It fills the knowledge gap by confirming the influence of higher education students’ perceived behaviour on their AI acceptance. Additionally, it uses SEM approach, which is very new in quantitative analysis, to test and establish the research model. © 2025, Emerald Publishing Limited.
