AU  - Yenduri, G.
AU  - Maddikunta, P.K.R.
AU  - Soufiene, B.O.
AU  - Gadekallu, T.R.
AB  - The transition of Industry 4.0 to Industry 5.0 has employed the integration of artificial intelligence, robots, and modern technologies to strengthen the traditional process in various domains by establishing a harmony between the robots and humans. Industry 5.0 requires an effective, seamless collaboration between the human and robots. The recent advancement in the AI-based distributed learning mechanism, i.e., federated learning (FL) enables the integration of data from multiple devices or components irrespective of their location by ensuring privacy. Thus to strengthen the capabilities of collaborative robots and to establish an effective, heterogeneous, safe production ecosystem, we have proposed a fusion of FL with collaborative robots in Industry 5.0 perspective. This research article presents the significance and impact of integrating federated learning in cobots considering the Industry 5.0 scenario on various aspects such as strengths challenges and applications. First, we present a detailed discussion on the federated learning architecture and existing FL-based solutions for robots. Next, we highlight the strengths of the FL-based cobots and their applications in various domains such as healthcare, production and manufacturing, education, supply-chain management, and disaster management. Finally, the challenges and future directions are discussed to improve the activities and operations of the FL-based cobots in an Industry 5.0 ecosystem. © The Institution of Engineering and Technology and its licensors 2025.
