TI  - Preparing social workers to resist coercive AI through social work education
AU  - Goldingay, S.
AU  - Hodgson, D.
AU  - Boddy, J.
AU  - Watts, L.
AU  - Nipperess, S.
AB  - Universities have used Artificial Intelligence (AI), machine learning and chatbots for several years. This has primarily included simple chatbots and programmes that monitor student engagement. Generative AI, however, has become more widespread with the public release of ChatGPT in late 2022, revolutionising how knowledge is generated, legitimised and shared. Generative AI raises questions for social work educators. How can we ethically prepare social work students for practice knowing that future technologies may regulate vulnerable populations under the guise of social support? How can we develop graduate skills that counter the inevitable risk of errors, bias and discrimination inherent in many AI tools? The prospect of generative AI tools being deployed at scale suggests that students and educators will likely use AI as a ubiquitous part of the teaching and learning process. The use of AI tools in practice is also likely to become part of the curriculum. This is of concern as some AI tools may coerce practitioners (and academics) to follow narrow and potentially unjust guidelines that cause harm. This chapter will explore some of the potentially harmful, coercive AI-assisted applications and their impacts. © 2026 selection and editorial matter, Goetz Ottmann and Carolyn Noble; individual chapters, the contributors.
