TI  - Exploring perceptions of early childhood teachers on the use of artificial intelligence in early childhood education
AU  - Lee, J.
AU  - Lee, J.O.
AU  - Yoon, J.
T2  - Journal of Early Childhood Teacher Education
AB  - This case study explores the perceptions of six early childhood teachers regarding Artificial Intelligence (AI) and its application in early childhood education. Researchers conducted individual interviews lasting about 20 to 30 minutes using semi-structured open-ended questions. The interview data revealed four key themes: (1) perception of AI, (2) feelings about AI, (3) AI’s roles in education, and (4) concerns/limitations and/or benefits of using AI. These themes align with the research questions of this study: (1) How do early childhood education teachers perceive AI? and (2) How do early childhood education teachers perceive the use of AI in education? Themes 1 and 2 addressed the first research question, while themes 3 and 4 addressed the second. The findings indicate that all six teachers believed AI could be useful in education, particularly for learning enhancement and classroom management. However, some teachers raised concerns about the need for adequate AI training and the potential for AI to make teachers obsolete. When discussing the use of AI in early childhood education, all teachers emphasized the importance of human interactions and expressed a preference for traditional learning methods that incorporate peer interactions, play, and concrete representations. © 2025 NAECTE.
