AB  - The study explores the advantages of using African indigenous languages as languages of instruction in African universities and to interrogate how the use of indigenous languages can improve fairness, transparency and honesty in the way students handle academic work. This study adopts decoloniality and technology appropriation model. To collect empirical data the study makes use of interviews to gather views and perspectives of purposively selected university lecturers from selected Zimbabwean universities. The findings show that use of African indigenous languages as languages of instruction in Zimbabwean universities is ideal and offers many benefits to both learner and teacher. Additionally, the use of African indigenous languages will neutralize the threat of AI in the Zimbabwe education sector. As such, this study calls for the adoption of African indigenous languages as a panacea to the ethical dilemmas that are exacerbated by the use of AI by university students © 2025 by IGI Global Scientific Publishing. All rights reserved.
