TI  - THE INFLUENCING FACTORS OF INTEGRATING ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE IN TEACHING FOR STEAM TEACHERS IN PRIMARY AND SECONDARY SCHOOLS: A Qualitative Study
AU  - Huang, Q.
AU  - Wang, Z.
AU  - Yang, J.
AU  - Wu, L.
AU  - Hung, W.L.D.
AB  - The deep integration of artificial intelligence and education stands as a crucial pathway for educational reform and innovation. Within the realm of STEAM education, the utilization and fusion of AI technologies have injected fresh impetus into teaching methodologies. Nonetheless, amidst the amalgamation of diverse AI tools and intricate educational components, STEAM educators encounter a myriad of challenges. This research employs a qualitative analysis grounded in theory to delve into comprehensive interviews with 18 STEAM instructors across primary and secondary educational institutions. Through processes like open coding, main coding, and selective coding on the interview data, the study synthesizes the perspectives, obstacles, and influential factors faced by STEAM educators in integrating intelligent technologies into their teaching practices. It emerges that while most STEAM teachers exhibit a positive stance, practical hurdles persist, including insufficient digital literacy among educators, inadequate teacher team cohesion, and limited backing and investment from school administrators. Furthermore, the research identifies three pivotal factors—teaching environment, social context, and educator proficiency—as critical influencers shaping the integration and utilization of intelligent technologies within STEAM instruction. Drawing from these findings, the study forwards several recommendations to underpin the cultivation of high-caliber AI-driven STEAM education. © 2026 selection and editorial matter, Stephen J. Farenga, Salvatore G. Garofalo, and Daniel Ness; individual chapters, the contributors.
