TI  - Artificial-Intelligence-Based Automated Instructor Feedback System for Education and Research
AU  - Yadav, V.
AU  - Tomar, P.
AB  - Artificial intelligence (AI)-based automated instructor feedback systems offer significant advantages in improving teaching and learning quality which help in enhancing the continuous professional growth of educators and researchers. Therefore, this chapter presents a systematic review of literature on automatic feedback and AI-based automated feedback systems for education and research to support instructors and researchers to enhance the quality of teaching and research. The integration of AI tools in education and research is significantly transforming the way learning, teaching and research are conducted. For learners, instructors, and researchers, AI-based platforms provide personalized learning experiences, adapting to individual needs, and enhancing engagement through personalized content and real-time feedback. By analysing classroom interactions, student performance, and instructional content, AI tools generate objective evaluations and suggest tailored professional development resources, enhancing teaching quality and effectiveness. In research, AI-based feedback mechanisms assist in optimizing research methodologies, improving experimental design, and enhancing the overall research process. Automated systems can analyse research outputs, identify areas for improvement, and recommend relevant literature and methodologies, thereby supporting researchers in producing high-quality, impactful work. The integration of AI tools in both education and research promotes an environment of continuous teaching and learning, leading to more effective learning and teaching, with innovative research for the academic community. However, the design and deployment of AI-based feedback systems require full attention on some ethical issues, such as feedback privacy of students and the education and research process. Therefore, based on this work, several important observations and recommendations are suggested for improving the education and research processes. © 2026 selection and editorial matter, K.M. Soni, Nitasha Hasteer, Aditi Bhardwaj, Rahul Sindhwani, and J., Paulo Davim; individual chapters, the contributors.
