TI  - Exploring Intelligent and Efficient Cognition for Radio Frequency Fingerprints
AU  - Bai, K.J.
AU  - Li, J.
T2  - Proceedings of SPIE - The International Society for Optical Engineering
AB  - In modern communication society, authenticating smart devices in a large pool of internet of things (IoT) networks becomes a critical measure to ensure the security and robustness of large communication system deployment. Artificial intelligence and machine learning (AI/ML) techniques, in recent years, have demonstrated their uniqueness when identifying wireless spectrum and encoded data symbols in noisy and interfering environment. To further exploit AI/ML's capability in communication workloads, here, we showcase a modular framework for deep learning models to reduce the computing complexity and required hardware resources when authenticating radio frequency (RF) signals at the edge. To be specific, a large-scale convolutional neural network (CNN) is partitioned into multiple sub-modules according to input sizes together with a shared input layer. Each individual sub-module is trained with their assigned agents while all weight values are aggregated to ensure the consistent of shared layer across sub-modules before the new training epoch takes place. Proof-of-concept demos were made to authenticate Wi-Fi transmitters based upon their RF fingerprints - raw in-phase and quadrature (I/Q) components. © 2025 SPIE. All rights reserved.
