AB  - There is a shortage of programs targeted at developing highly skilled manpower at higher educational institutions for meeting the needs of semiconductor and electronics industry. Conventional lecture based approach to learning doesn't provide the opportunity for hands-on training which inhibits the students from entering electronics industry after graduation. This article discusses about transforming students who have undergone traditional learning in their bachelors to electronics industry-ready through experiential learning. Experiential learning is practiced in School of Electronic Systems and Automation at Digital University Kerala. Students were given hands-on activities in lab throughout the course. Students learned from the data sheet based on the specifications of the product. They worked individually on some projects and as a team in student initiated start-ups. Over a period of time, students developed strong concepts and multiple job-oriented skills. An analysis of experiential learning outcome is performed by conducting a survey among students and by a comparison of their performance parameters, the result of which turned out to be positive and is discussed in this article. In addition, we also list various supporting activities including that with professional societies and outreach, that led to high student engagement beyond the university. This also resulted in direct and in-direct means of experience sharing with multiple institutions. © 2024 IEEE.
