TI  - Exploring the Impact of AI-Driven Virtual Assistants in AR and VR Environments for Special Needs Education: A Quantitative Analysis
AU  - Mohamed, A.
AU  - Faisal, R.
AU  - Shaalan, K.
T2  - Lecture Notes in Computer Science
AB  - This study investigates the complex interaction between hypotheses and variables in the context of deploying AI-powered virtual assistants in augmented reality (AR) and virtual reality (VR) environments for special needs schooling. The research investigates how various independent variables, including the characteristics of AI-driven virtual assistants, development and implementation aspects, contributions from educational institutions or technology developers, and initiatives or tactics used, impact dependent variables such as improved educational outcomes for children with special needs, a transformation of the learning experience, and the evolution of AI-powered. Quantitative analysis results show substantial relationships between the characteristics and functions of AI-powered virtual assistants, development factors, and educational outcomes for kids with special needs. These findings highlight the potential of modern technology to improve educational experiences and outcomes, lending support to the concept that the qualities of virtual assistants play a critical role in determining educational outcomes. Furthermore, qualitative insights emphasize the need of stakeholder participation, institutional support, and specific methods for effective integration, as well as addressing problems such as resource restrictions and technical barriers. Overall, this study adds vital insights to the field of special needs education by giving evidence-based guidelines for educators, policymakers, and technology developers on how to use AI-powered technologies to construct inclusive and accessible learning environments. The project intends to stimulate continuous improvement in educational practices and policy by explaining the elements that influence the integration and impact of AI-powered virtual assistants, thereby helping students with special needs to thrive and achieve in the digital era. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2024.
