TI  - Advanced Nanomaterials for Diabetes Monitoring and Therapeutics
AU  - Ali, S.M.
AU  - Emran, K.M.
AU  - Okasha, R.M.
AB  - Nanomaterials with their extraordinary characteristics have considerably contributed to the medicinal research biosphere regarding chronic disease diagnostics and therapeutics. Recent advancements within this field incorporate the utilization of nanomaterials, accompanied by polymeric scaffolds, in order to detect and remedy the blood glucose level of diabetic patients. Glucose sensors can be broadly divided into enzymatic and nonenzymatic glucose sensing. Enzymatic glucose sensing, based on nanomaterials, is characterized by high sensitivity and selectivity, while the nonenzymatic glucose sensing exhibits higher sensitivity, wider linear range, low operation potential, and high selectivity. On the other hand, this class of applications facilitate the oral administration of insulin, which is superior to the traditional administration of insulin through injection. However, several obstacles were encountered in this therapy, including low stability/degradation, pH sensitivity, and low biostability. This chapter addresses the present and recent research reports regarding the development of nanomaterials incorporating polymeric matrices that are employed in diabetic diagnostics and oral/pulmonary treatment.
