TY  - CHAP
TI  - Multimodal Classification of the Arabic Alphabet: An Artificial Intelligence Approach for Handwriting, Sign Language, and Braille
AU  - Rouabhi, S.
AU  - Tlemsani, R.
AB  - This chapter examines the application of artificial intelligence techniques for multimodal classification of the Arabic alphabet across three key domains: handwriting, sign language, and braille. Each modality employs a specialized methodology tailored to its unique requirements. For handwritten Arabic character recognition, a hybrid model combining particle swarm optimization and a support vector classifier achieves a test accuracy of 90.20%. Arabic sign language alphabet classification utilizes MediaPipe for hand landmark detection alongside Inception ResNet v2 for feature extraction, followed by a custom neural network, achieving a test accuracy of 93.77%. Arabic braille character recognition employs EfficientNet B3 for feature extraction with a simple neural network for classification, resulting in a macro-average F1-score of 81.33%. These AI-driven approaches underscore the potential for enhancing Arabic language education and accessibility, offering valuable support for learners with diverse needs and abilities. © 2025 by IGI Global Scientific Publishing. All rights reserved.
