TI  - Rethinking AI in Education: A Comparative Study of Custom and External Systems
AU  - Basaran, B.
AB  - Artificial intelligence (AI) has rapidly emerged as a crucial tool across diverse sectors, including healthcare, finance, and education [1], [2]. In education, AI often conjures images of innovative products from leading brands. However, there are significant drawbacks to relying on paid or free AI services with user interfaces. These include prohibitive costs, accessibility issues due to unstable internet connections, and data privacy concerns. Moreover, the one-size-fits-all approach of many AI services fails to meet the diverse needs of educational environments, compromising flexibility and customization. Dependence on external service providers for updates and consistent operation introduces risks of educational interruptions. To address these limitations and explore AI's potential in foreign language teaching, this research investigates the development of a local AI system. Such systems offer increased control, customization tailored to individual or institutional needs, and heightened privacy, suggesting a shift toward more autonomous and tailored educational technology solutions. This study aims to compare the results of a local Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) AI model with those of the paid service, ChatGPT, in identifying gender-specific profession names in schoolbooks. This comparison serves as a test to evaluate the efficacy and accuracy of each model for this specific task. The efficiency of the AI models will be examined by analyzing the relationship between gender and professions within a sample database created using five randomly selected German language textbooks used in Turkish schools. The purpose is to demonstrate the potential of a locally installed AI system for creating databases for foreign language teaching, overcoming the limitations of relying on external AI services, and providing a more customizable, efficient, and privacy-conscious solution for language learning. This study is supported by the Scientific Research Coordination Unit of Anadolu University under project number SBA-2024-2457. © 2024 IEEE.
