AU  - Misra, S.
AU  - Ghosh, A.
AB  - The agriculture sector is undergoing a transformation driven by new technologies, which seems very promising as it will enable this primary sector to move to the next level of farm productivity and profitability. Due to constraints from climate, soils, hydrology, plants, animals, forests, and human health, traditional agriculture many times fail to secure food. Modern agriculture has enabled more people to eat better and more food worldwide. One such farming practice is precision farming, which entails applying inputs (what is needed) when and where it is needed, and it has emerged as the third wave of the contemporary agriculture revolution (the first two waves were mechanization and the green revolution with its genetic modification). Modern agricultural technologies had raised operational and net earnings. In order to ensure the sustainability of farm produce, new technologies are also increasingly being employed in farms when the environment is taken into account. The adoption of these technologies does, however, come with risks and trade-offs. However, factors such as improved farmer education and training, information exchange, simple access to financial resources, and rising consumer demand for organic food will make it easier for farmers to embrace sustainable farming technology. © 2024 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
