TI  - K Means Clustering for Evaluating Practice Modes in an AI Education Application Course
AU  - Li, Y.
AB  - As AI’s influence extends from precision medicine to digital art, educators face the dual challenge of conveying rigorous theory and fostering hands-on fluency with real tools. In this study, we design, implement, and critically evaluate a multi-modal practice model for a university “AI Education Application” course, informed by direct student feedback. We surveyed 215 undergraduates, asking them to self-assess their proficiency in five experiential activities-slide-deck design, short-video production, poster creation, mind-map generation, and natural-language-processing exercises-and then standardized these ratings to remove scale bias. Employing K-Means clustering, with the silhouette method pinpointing k=3 as optimal, we uncovered three distinct learner archetypes: a “Multimedia Champions” group excelling in visual and video tasks, a “Generalist Collaborators” cohort with balanced skills across all modes, and a “Foundational Learners” segment in need of extra support for more technical exercises. Beyond segmentation, we translated these insights into concrete course refinements-forming complementary project teams, curating specialized resource libraries, and delivering tailored feedback pathways-to heighten engagement and learning outcomes. In my view, embedding unsupervised machine learning into curriculum evaluation not only surfaces hidden patterns in student behavior but also offers a reproducible, data-driven blueprint for continuously refining AI education. I’m excited by how this approach bridges pedagogical vision and analytics, and I believe it charts a promising path toward truly adaptive, learner-centered AI courses. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
