AU  - Setiawati, I.
AU  - Mulyono, H.
AU  - Busthami Nur, A.H.B.
T2  - Multidisciplinary Reviews
AB  - The advancement of digital technology has transformed the global educational landscape, particularly by enhancing 21st-century skills such as critical thinking and creative thinking, as well as improving the implementation of digital assessments. Although extensive research has been conducted in this area, there is a lack of comprehensive bibliometric mapping that examines citation trends, research collaboration patterns, and the conceptual structure of these topics within the context of digital learning. This study aims to map publication trends, citation patterns, international collaboration networks, and dominant research themes, while also visualizing the relationships among critical thinking, creative thinking, and digital assessment from 2014 to 2024. This bibliometric study utilized data from the Scopus database, following the PRISMA 2020 protocol, and focused on open-access articles in social sciences, arts, and humanities. Thirteen thousand six hundred articles were analyzed using VOSviewer software for descriptive analysis, collaboration network visualization, bibliographic coupling, and keyword co-occurrence. The findings indicate a significant rise in publications and citations since 2019, driven by the digitalization of education during the COVID-19 pandemic. Key themes included AI literacy, the Metaverse in education, and generative AI. Developed countries have mainly led research collaboration, but nations like Indonesia and Malaysia have shown notable growth recently. Critical thinking emerged as the central theme in keyword co-occurrence analysis, linked to higher education, learning, digital literacy, and artificial intelligence. Emerging topics like ChatGPT and adaptive learning are increasingly prevalent in research. This study provides a foundation for future digital education research and offers opportunities for international collaboration and curriculum development using advanced technologies in higher education. © 2026, Malque Publishing. All rights reserved.
