AU  - Cho, N.
AU  - Li, H.
AU  - Zhang, X.
AU  - Li, Y.
T2  - Journal of Science Education and Technology
AB  - This paper investigates the effectiveness of a platform that combines computer simulations and a generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) chatbot, focusing on teacher-chatbot interactions, the quality of chatbot responses, and teachers’ perceptions, assessed through the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM). While GenAI chatbots have been explored in educational settings, studies integrating them with computer simulations—an effective tool for learning science—remain limited. To address this gap, the AiSim platform was developed, incorporating PhET computer simulations and the Doubao GenAI chatbot. An exploratory study was conducted in secondary school physics education, focusing on two topics: energy and DC circuits. Data were collected from 12 secondary school physics teachers in Beijing. The findings reveal that most of the provided questions were understanding and remembering questions. Moreover, teachers primarily posed single-step questions without follow-up queries, limiting deeper interactions—a key advantage of GenAI chatbots. Additionally, the average score of GenAI chatbot responses was satisfactory for most question types. The results from the questionnaire and focus group indicated that participants had a positive attitude toward the platform, though concerns were raised about platform limitations, implementation challenges, and teacher readiness. The study concludes with implications for further developing GenAI chatbots to enhance physics education through computer simulations. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Nature B.V. 2025.
