AB  - This study presents a strategic framework to enhance academic performance in STEM disciplines, using personalized, outcome-based curriculum design for second-year Information Technology students in Bangkok, Thailand. Addressing the limitations of traditional curricula, a course recommendation system was developed using student profiles, course metadata, and intended learning outcomes aligned with the Thai Qualifications Framework (TQF). Integrated with Microsoft Copilot, the system supports personalized academic planning and data-driven decision-making. A pilot with 278 students showed that 85% improved their performance, with an average grade increase of 8%, and over 90% reported satisfaction with their learning plans. While the system effectively supports student-centered learning, its reliance on structured digital data presents challenges for broader implementation in resource-limited institutions. This framework offers a scalable, context-aware model for integrating intelligent curriculum planning tools in higher education, supporting both academic success and alignment with SDG 4 goals. © 2025 IEEE.
