AB  - Educators working at the “jagged technological frontier” of generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) (Dell’Acqua, F., McFowland, E., Mollick, E. R., Lifshitz-Assaf, H., Kellogg, K., Rajendran, S., Krayer, L., Candelon, F., & Lakhani, K. R. (2023). Navigating the Jagged Technological frontier: Field experimental evidence of the effects of AI on knowledge worker productivity and quality. SSRN Electronic Journal. https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.4573321" xmlns:xlink=“https://www.w3.org/1999/xlink”>https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.4573321) are exploring the possibilities and capacities of these emerging technologies. Many are leveraging the participatory affordances of social media platforms to gather, disseminate, explore, caution, and inquire within self-organized professional learning communities. This exploratory study, focusing on K-12 educators, is part of a broader research initiative that is investigating educational applications of GenAI tools, such as ChatGPT, in K-20 contexts. This paper reports results from a qualitative content analysis of posts in a Facebook group, ChatGPT for Teachers. The findings represent a snapshot of early adopters’ interactions in pedagogical experimentation, knowledge co-construction, and innovative instructional practices, which can inform educators, educational leaders, and policymakers. © 2025 selection and editorial matter, Helen Crompton and Diane Burke; individual chapters, the contributors.
